.TH "XmeUseColorObj" "library call"
.nh
.SH "NAME"
\fB\%XmeUseColorObj\fP,
\fB\%XmeGetColorObjData\fP,
\fB\%XmeGetPixelData\fP,
\fB\%XmeGetIconControlInfo\fP,
\fB\%XmeGetDesktopColorCells\fP \(em Widget writer functions that query the desktop colour server
.iX "XmeUseColorObj"
.iX "Xme color functions" "XmeUseColorObj"
.iX "Xme color functions" "XmeGetColorObjData"
.iX "Xme color functions" "XmeGetPixelData"
.iX "Xme color functions" "XmeGetIconControlInfo"
.iX "Xme color functions" "XmeGetDesktopColorCells"
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.nf
#include <Xm/ColorObjP\&.h>
.PP
\fBBoolean \fBXmeUseColorObj\fP\fR(\fBvoid\fR);
.PP
\fBBoolean \fBXmeGetColorObjData\fP\fR(
\fBScreen *\fBscreen\fR\fR,
\fBint *\fBcolorUse\fR\fR,
\fBXmPixelSet *\fBpixelSet\fR\fR,
\fBunsigned short \fBnum_pixelSet\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBactive_id\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBinactive_id\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBprimary_id\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBsecondary_id\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBtext_id\fR\fR);
\fBBoolean \fBXmeGetPixelData\fP\fR(
\fBint \fBscreen_number\fR\fR,
\fBint *\fBcolorUse\fR\fR,
\fBXmPixelSet *\fBpixelSet\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBa\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBi\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBp\fR\fR,
\fBshort *\fBs\fR\fR);
.PP
\fBBoolean \fBXmeGetIconControlInfo\fP\fR(
\fBScreen *\fBscreen\fR\fR,
\fBBoolean *\fBuseMaskRtn\fR\fR,
\fBBoolean *\fBuseMultiColorIconsRtn\fR\fR,
\fBBoolean *\fBuseIconFileCacheRtn\fR\fR);
.PP
\fBBoolean \fBXmeGetDesktopColorCells\fP\fR(
\fBScreen *\fBscreen\fR\fR,
\fBColormap \fBcolormap\fR\fR,
\fBXColor *\fBcolors\fR\fR,
\fBint \fBn_colors\fR\fR,
\fBint *\fBncolors_returns\fR\fR);
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
On a CDE desktop, the session manager acts as a colour server: it
allocates the colours of the palette of the user and publishes them,
and Motif applications take their colours from it (the ColorObj, an
object Motif creates for the application, reads them unless its
\fB\%useColorObj\fP resource is False)\&.  These
functions give access to that data\&.  They all return False when no
colour server is running or the application does not use it\&.
.PP
\fB\%XmeUseColorObj\fP tells whether the application uses the colour
server\&.
.PP
\fB\%XmeGetColorObjData\fP returns the pixel sets of the palette of
\fIscreen\fP: \fIcolorUse\fP receives the colour mode
(\fB\%XmCO_BLACK_WHITE\fP,
\fB\%XmCO_LOW_COLOR\fP, \fB\%XmCO_MEDIUM_COLOR\fP or
\fB\%XmCO_HIGH_COLOR\fP), up to
\fInum_pixelSet\fP entries (at most \fB\%XmCO_NUM_COLORS\fP, 8) of
\fIpixelSet\fP
receive the foreground, background, top shadow, bottom shadow and
select pixels (\fB\%fg\fP, \fB\%bg\fP, \fB\%ts\fP, \fB\%bs\fP and
\fB\%sc\fP) of each
colour set, and the other arguments the colour set used for active
and inactive window frames, primary and secondary areas, and text\&.
\fB\%XmeGetPixelData\fP is the same for the screen numbered
\fIscreen_number\fP, with all \fB\%XmCO_NUM_COLORS\fP sets and without
the text
set\&.  The return arguments may be NULL, except \fIpixelSet\fP\&.
.PP
\fB\%XmeGetIconControlInfo\fP returns the icon settings of the colour
server: whether icons use their masks, whether they are drawn in
colour, and whether the directories of icon files are cached (see
\fB\%XmGetIconFileName\fP)\&.  Without a colour server all three are
True\&.
.PP
\fB\%XmeGetDesktopColorCells\fP is for an application that uses a
private
colormap: it fills \fIcolors\fP with up to \fIn_colors\fP colours used
by the
desktop (pixel and RGB value), the most important first, so that the
application can allocate the same colours in its own colormap and
avoid colormap flashing; \fIncolors_returns\fP receives the number of
colours\&.  It also enters the primary, secondary and
text sets in the colour cache of \fIcolormap\fP\&.  It returns False for
a
black and white palette\&.
.SH "RETURN"
.PP
The functions return True if a colour server is in use and the data was
returned, and False otherwise\&.
.SH "RELATED"
.PP
\fB\%XmeGetDefaultPixel\fP(3), \fB\%XmGetColors\fP(3) and
\fB\%XmGetIconFileName\fP(3)\&.
